    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d335">

                <pb facs="0617" n="585" xml:id="pg585"/>

                <head>335. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Jordan<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d335-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-09-26T17:40:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 26,
                                1958—5:40 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>902. Embtel 970.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d335-f2"/>FYI. Department shares Embassy
                    view that Jordan cannot for long maintain present stance toward Arab neighbors
                    and that some form of accommodation will take place. While we not seeking
                    maintain HKJ as western fortress in Middle East, any abrupt or forceful change
                    in Jordan’s status would be likely precipitate hostilities in area. We are
                    therefore concerned that political future HKJ be determined by agreement between
                    parties concerned and that any changes which occur should be brought about in
                    orderly and peaceful manner.</p>
                <p>Our attitude toward successor government would depend on its composition, policy
                    toward US and West and manner its coming to power. End FYI.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Herter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
